  "notes": "State, daily and hourly are core snapshots (hourly datetimes moved, below). twice_daily has no snapshot: it is built from the same fixture (forecast_nl.json) through the code, which serves the library's raw day and night list unchanged (daily is that list with only the daytime entries, the first day borrowing the following night's low). now is the clock the state test froze at. Changes for realism (test-data artefact): the forecast test froze at 2025-09-22 instead, so core's hourly datetimes (worked out from that clock) fell in September 2025 while the readings, their is_daytime flags and the daily dates are December 2023's. Hourly is moved -634 days to start at the hour under way, keeping each local time of day, and written with Brussels' winter offset +01:00 instead of the summer +02:00 the September clock gave it. Daily and twice-daily datetimes are bare dates (YYYY-MM-DD); a night entry carries the next day's date. Text is in the configured language (Dutch here). templow is absent when the API gives no low, precipitation_probability is null when it gives no chance, and wind_bearing is null when the wind is variable.",
